{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Then Israel sang, etc.</b> One needs to know what the nature of this song is, and also why they did not sing a song over the manna as they sang a song over the water, and the entire passage requires explanation. And it is possible that they said this song about the Torah, and with this as well no challenge can be raised against that generation — why they did not sing a new song when the Torah was given to them, the heritage (Deuteronomy 33:4) to which praise is fitting — for certainly this is the song of the Torah, which is called a well of water: it is called a well after the source, which is the supernal Shekhinah that is called a well, and also because the Torah is likened to water. And His saying <b>Spring up, O well</b> is in the language of ascent, for they are not speaking of a well below but of a supernal well — meaning, a lofty one. <b>Answer to it</b> in the manner of what is said (Exodus 15:21): ״וַתַּעַן לָהֶם מִרְיָם״, which is the way of song." "And His saying <b>The princes dug it, etc.</b> — it may be explained according to their words (Genesis Rabbah 64:8), who said that through the pursuit of Torah one repairs the supernal source called a well, and the repair is according to the level of those engaged in Torah. The early righteous ones who were on a great level — they are the ones who dug the well and made it fit to drink from, and they are the patriarchs, in the sod of (Genesis 29:2,3) ״וְהָאֶבֶן גְּדוֹלָה עַל פִּי הַבְּאֵר וַיָּגֶל אֶת הָאֶבֶן וַיַּשְׁקְ אֶת הַצֹּאן״, and from then on the Torah was fit to be given to Israel, yet it was still not in a condition of attainment to drink from it until the nobles of the people dug it — they are Moses, who brought it down to us, and after him stood those who received from him: elders and prophets and the men of the Great Assembly, and they explained it and revealed its hidden things. And this is His saying <b>The nobles of the people dug it</b>, for the Written Torah without the Oral Torah, a person cannot drink from its waters." "And His saying <b>with a lawgiver, with their staffs</b>, is that which the sages of the generations added as innovation in Torah, and this is nothing but engraving an engraving in it, and it is not like the measure of the early ones who dug and carved out; and even this is only with their staffs — of the early ones — for every precise deduction and deduction that one deduces in Torah must be explained according to their words, and anything that is not founded upon the words of the early ones cannot be relied upon." "And His saying <b>and from the wilderness to Mattanah</b> — meaning, what its inheritors merited, they merited only because they considered themselves like a wilderness, as we explained in the verse (Exodus 19:2) ״וַיַּחֲנוּ בַּמִּדְבָּר״ — that the Torah is not acquired except through humility and lowliness. And His saying <b>and from Mattanah to Nahaliel</b> means that through the Torah that was given to us, El took us as a heritage, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:9) ״יַעֲקֹב חֶבֶל נַחֲלָתוֹ״. It further intends that El took us as a heritage to be uniquely designated for us as our Elohim, as it is written (Psalms 135:4) ״כִּי יַעֲקֹב בָּחַר לוֹ יָהּ״. And also that He dwelt within us and abandoned His supernal dwelling." "And His saying <b>and from Nahaliel to Bamoth</b> — meaning, and from the midst of this matter — that El took us as a heritage — to Bamoth (heights), we attained a supernal level above the angels, for HaShem chose us over them. And His saying <b>and from the height of the valley, which</b> etc., may be explained according to what has preceded us: that the ultimate purpose of a person's reward in his commandments and his happiness is only in the supernal world, but in this world it is not the place for the attainment of spiritual happiness." "<b>And now</b> it is a necessity for a person to depart from this world in order to receive his recompense from the good hand of HaShem. And what we see — that death was brought about by the sin of the person — this itself tells that but for this, the person would have lived forever. You will say: if so, the person would be lacking his good recompense. Therefore the masters of sod said that but for the sin, the person would have ascended to the firmament and dwelled in the upper chamber and in the courtyard. And this is your sign: Elijah the prophet, who ascended heavenward to receive his recompense and did not taste the taste of death. And as for the body — it is true that it cannot stand in the supreme upper chamber; even though the body is refined and stands in the category of the spiritual, nevertheless relative to supreme spirituality it is considered materiality. But they of blessed memory said (Zohar vol. 1, 209a) regarding the matter of Elijah of blessed memory, that when he reached the sphere of the sun, there he divested himself of his body and left it in the sphere of the sun, and when he descends below he puts it on and descends in it. And they of blessed memory said further that when HaShem gave the Torah to Israel at Mount Sinai, Israel became free men from death, and they returned to be like the person before he sinned, as it is written: ״חָרוּת עַל הַלֻּחוֹת״, and they of blessed memory said (Shemot Rabbah 41:7): do not read חָרוּת (engraved) but חֵרוּת (freedom) — freedom from the angel of death. And since this is so, at the time when the consummation of their perfection arrived, they would ascend body and soul and divest their bodies in the sphere of the sun like Elijah. And this is the meaning of the verse here, <b>מִבָּמוֹת הַגַּיְא</b> — meaning: from the supreme degree that we attain through the agency of the Torah, there is yet another attainment from the side of this thing, which is הַגַּיְא (the valley) — namely, the descent of our physicality into the valley, which is the grave — <b>אֲשֶׁר בִּשְׂדֵה מוֹאָב</b>, for the earth is called a field (שָׂדֶה), and it says <b>מוֹאָב</b> — for the Father (אַב) of the world created it." "<b>Or</b> it alludes to the woman, who is also called a field (שָׂדֶה), as stated in their words (Zohar vol. 1, 36a). And the intention is along these lines: הַגַּיְא — which came from the side of the woman, who is Chava, who came from the Father of all created beings, for HaShem took her from his ribs; and there is no woman in the world who came in this manner from the ribs of a person except this one. And she was the cause of death, to cast bodies into the valley (הַגַּיְא). From now on, through the agency of the attainment of the Torah, the matter will return to its prior state before Chava sinned, and the body will be as it was in the גַּיְא above." "And I do not know the boundary of this place that it calls ״רֹאשׁ הַפִּסְגָּה״, for we said the place is the sphere of the sun, yet it says <b>רֹאשׁ הַפִּסְגָּה</b> — perhaps it is higher, in the place of the souls. Therefore it says <b>וְנִשְׁקָפָה</b> etc. — upon the face of this world, which is called יְשִׁימוֹן, meaning desolation (שְׁמָמָה), for it is destined to become desolate. Or it is called thus because the evil impulse, which desolates the created beings, holds sway in it. And whatever place looks out upon the face of this world — that is the sphere of the sun; there will be deposited what was worthy to be in the valley (הַגַּיְא). And it is found that through the agency of the Torah's benefits, as required, it will be attained to bequeath life to the soul and the body." "<b>End of the section of Ḥukkat</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The phrase לֹא נִקְרָא תִּגָּר (lit. 'no merchandise was called out,' i.e., no challenge or indictment) is translated as 'no challenge can be raised,' following the idiom as used in rabbinic argumentation.", 5 "I read בָּעֲלִיָּה וּבֶחָצֵר as 'in the upper chamber and in the courtyard' based on the parallel Temple/heavenly architecture idiom; the term עֲלִיָּה denotes an upper chamber or story."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:29.618644809Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}